<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward III of England</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1312">1312</a>–<a href="/years/1377">1377</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-plantagenet">House of Plantagenet</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> reigned from 1327 to 1377. Claiming the French crown, he began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>, won the battles of Sluys and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Crécy</a>, and took Calais after a long siege in 1346–1347. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bretigny">Treaty of Brétigny</a> of 1360 gave him Aquitaine, Ponthieu and Calais in full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, but most of these gains were lost by his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england.jpg" alt="Edward III of England" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Bruges · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_III_of_England_(Order_of_the_Garter).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Flanders was a hinge of politics between England and France: English wool exports and Flemish alliances shaped the opening of the Hundred Years’ War, and <b>Edward III</b> was proclaimed king of France at Ghent in 1340.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Philippa of Hainaut, queen of <b>Edward III</b>, took the Valenciennes chronicler Jean Froissart into her service, giving him access to the English court for his chronicles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Florentine merchant-bankers, the Bardi, Peruzzi and Acciaiuoli, lent to popes and kings and collected papal revenues across Europe, until loans to <b>Edward III of England</b> and others helped bring them down in the 1340s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Scottish-held town of Berwick-upon-Tweed surrendered to King <b>Edward III of England</b> after a four-month siege, once a Scottish relief army had been heavily defeated at Halidon Hill.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-20">20 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Edward III of England</b> and the captive John II of France agreed the Treaty of Brétigny, ending the first phase of the Hundred Years' War.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-8">8 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The English kings held Ponthieu as vassals of France; <b>Edward III</b> fought at Crécy, inside the county, in 1346, and the Treaty of Brétigny of 1360 gave it to him in full sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-ponthieu">County of Ponthieu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Windsor Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Richmond Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.48,-0.6&amp;year=1354" aria-label="Windsor Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4536 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Edward III of England"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4986 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4986 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.48,-0.6&amp;year=1354">What was here in 1354? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…say to them that they send no more to me for any adventure that falleth, as long as my son is alive: and also say to them that they suffer him this day to win his spurs…&#8221;</p><footer>Words at Crécy, 1346, of his son the Black Prince (Froissart, Chronicles) &#183; tr. John Bourchier, Lord Berners, 1525 &#183; Froissart's account &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/basis/froissart-full.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have good cause here to abide them, for I am on the right heritage of the queen my mother, the which land was given at her marriage: I will challenge it of mine adversary Philip of Valois.&#8221;</p><footer>Words to his army before Crécy, 1346 (Froissart, Chronicles) &#183; tr. John Bourchier, Lord Berners, 1525 &#183; Froissart's account; he stood in Ponthieu, his mother's inheritance &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/basis/froissart-full.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
